1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,614
You could turn the Sahara green.

2
00:00:02,055 --> 00:00:06,861
Geological records suggest it
has happened more than 230 times before.

3
00:00:07,245 --> 00:00:10,553
But this time, what you'd
change thousands of miles away

4
00:00:10,832 --> 00:00:12,887
is the part that's easy to miss.

5
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:16,973
The Sahara is the world's
largest non-polar desert.

6
00:00:17,298 --> 00:00:22,384
In some areas, it gets just a few inches
of rain a year, tens of millimeters.

7
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:24,729
So could we really cover it in green?

8
00:00:24,973 --> 00:00:27,678
Engineers have already
done it, in patches.

9
00:00:28,003 --> 00:00:31,683
And climate models have tested
what happens if we go all the way.

10
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:35,561
But first, the Sahara
that was already green.

11
00:00:36,258 --> 00:00:39,625
According to one reconstruction
of the last green period,

12
00:00:39,706 --> 00:00:44,791
between roughly 11,000 and 6,000
years ago much of the Sahara

13
00:00:44,872 --> 00:00:49,667
was grassland and savanna,
dotted with lakes and wetlands.

14
00:00:50,108 --> 00:00:54,659
Other records place the wet phase
a few thousand years earlier or later.

15
00:00:54,868 --> 00:00:56,970
Not a jungle: savanna.

16
00:00:57,922 --> 00:01:03,437
In north-central Africa, about 7,000
years ago, a lake called Mega-Chad

17
00:01:03,646 --> 00:01:08,696
spanned an area larger than all
of the Great Lakes combined, NASA says.

18
00:01:09,079 --> 00:01:12,852
Geologists think it was the world's
largest lake at the time.

19
00:01:13,293 --> 00:01:18,773
At a site called Gobero, in Niger,
archaeologists found about 200 burials

20
00:01:18,855 --> 00:01:24,439
beside a vanished lake, alongside
the remains of crocodiles and large fish.

21
00:01:25,043 --> 00:01:28,863
In Algeria's Tassili n'Ajjer,
a World Heritage site,

22
00:01:29,107 --> 00:01:33,600
there are more than 15,000 prehistoric
etchings and illustrations.

23
00:01:34,111 --> 00:01:35,016
How much wetter?

24
00:01:35,457 --> 00:01:39,672
During the last green period,
one reconstruction puts rainfall

25
00:01:39,753 --> 00:01:43,747
at around 100 millimeters
a year, about 4 inches,

26
00:01:44,072 --> 00:01:49,378
in the heart of the northeastern
Sahara, and around 450 millimeters,

27
00:01:49,622 --> 00:01:53,407
roughly 18 inches, near 18 degrees north.

28
00:01:53,848 --> 00:01:57,017
Those are rough estimates,
without error bars.

29
00:01:57,401 --> 00:01:59,804
And a piece of that world is still here.

30
00:02:00,129 --> 00:02:05,086
In northern Chad, the Lakes of Ounianga
sit in the middle of the desert,

31
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:08,964
fed by groundwater stored
during the last wet period.

32
00:02:09,347 --> 00:02:12,842
Pollen preserved in the sediments
of the region's original lake,

33
00:02:13,016 --> 00:02:16,882
which was about 50 meters, 160 feet, deep,

34
00:02:17,091 --> 00:02:22,397
suggests a wooded grassland, with plants
that now grow about 300 kilometers,

35
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:25,636
about 190 miles, farther south.

36
00:02:26,019 --> 00:02:28,039
So what turned the Sahara green?

37
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:31,174
Not people. Earth's orbit.

38
00:02:31,499 --> 00:02:33,798
Earth's orbit has a slow wobble,

39
00:02:33,972 --> 00:02:37,303
a cycle that plays out
over many thousands of years.

40
00:02:37,687 --> 00:02:41,529
It changes how much summer sunlight
reaches the Northern Hemisphere.

41
00:02:41,971 --> 00:02:46,278
When northern summers were sunnier,
the West African monsoon pushed

42
00:02:46,324 --> 00:02:50,156
its rains far north, deep into the Sahara.

43
00:02:50,156 --> 00:02:53,349
But sunlight alone
may not explain how wet it got.

44
00:02:53,952 --> 00:02:57,494
Climate models struggle to produce
enough rain to fill all the lakes

45
00:02:57,528 --> 00:03:00,280
that geologists think the Sahara once had,

46
00:03:00,605 --> 00:03:04,123
and researchers are still working
out what the models are missing.

47
00:03:04,564 --> 00:03:09,870
One proposed amplifier is feedback:
more lakes and plants could mean more rain,

48
00:03:10,044 --> 00:03:12,180
and more rain, more plants.

49
00:03:12,563 --> 00:03:14,374
And it's not a one-time event.

50
00:03:14,816 --> 00:03:19,912
Geological records, mainly layers
of Mediterranean seabed mud laid down

51
00:03:19,970 --> 00:03:23,198
when African monsoon
floods surged into the sea,

52
00:03:23,523 --> 00:03:28,596
point to more than 230 green Sahara
periods in the last 8 million years.

53
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,798
That's a reconstruction,
not 230 events anyone observed.

54
00:03:34,309 --> 00:03:35,725
How did the last one end?

55
00:03:36,236 --> 00:03:39,800
Scientists still debate
whether it was abrupt or gradual.

56
00:03:40,125 --> 00:03:42,587
One reconstruction, built from dated lake,

57
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:45,292
marsh and river sediments
across the Sahara,

58
00:03:45,536 --> 00:03:51,259
finds the monsoon rains retreating southward
over about two to three thousand years.

59
00:03:51,538 --> 00:03:54,684
Other records have been read
as showing a more abrupt end.

60
00:03:55,009 --> 00:03:56,669
The desert crept south.

61
00:03:56,913 --> 00:04:00,501
Today, the Sahara sits
under a belt of sinking air.

62
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:06,004
Near the equator, warm, moist air
rises, and forms clouds and rain.

63
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:08,849
High up, it flows toward the poles,

64
00:04:08,965 --> 00:04:12,947
then sinks around 30 degrees
latitude, by then dry.

65
00:04:13,644 --> 00:04:17,626
NASA notes that descending
air inhibits cloud formation,

66
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:21,991
which is why clouds are rare and deserts
are common at those latitudes.

67
00:04:22,432 --> 00:04:25,718
So greening the Sahara isn't
just about finding water.

68
00:04:25,962 --> 00:04:28,667
You'd be planting
against the atmosphere itself.

69
00:04:28,992 --> 00:04:33,891
And yet, from orbit, you can find
green in the Sahara right now.

70
00:04:34,332 --> 00:04:37,664
This is East Oweinat, in southern Egypt.

71
00:04:38,268 --> 00:04:43,238
In a 1984 Landsat image, this area
is almost entirely barren.

72
00:04:43,679 --> 00:04:47,928
Today, it's covered in green circles,
each about half a mile across,

73
00:04:48,137 --> 00:04:51,039
watered by rotating sprinkler arms.

74
00:04:51,039 --> 00:04:52,676
The water comes from below.

75
00:04:53,059 --> 00:04:55,811
It's pumped
from the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer,

76
00:04:56,020 --> 00:05:01,871
a vast store of fossil groundwater
shared by Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.

77
00:05:02,382 --> 00:05:07,688
NASA says that water soaked into the sandstone
anywhere from 10,000 to a million

78
00:05:07,757 --> 00:05:13,040
years ago, when the region got more rain,
and that the aquifer recharges slowly

79
00:05:13,284 --> 00:05:16,314
and is considered
a non-renewable resource today.

80
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:21,852
Across the border in Libya, the Kufra
oasis runs on the same kind of water.

81
00:05:22,235 --> 00:05:26,380
In the 1980s, Libya began building
the Great Man-Made River

82
00:05:26,461 --> 00:05:28,203
to pipe it north to the coast.

83
00:05:28,481 --> 00:05:32,034
And at Toshka, Egypt let
water from Lake Nasser spill

84
00:05:32,104 --> 00:05:34,147
into natural basins in the desert.

85
00:05:34,588 --> 00:05:38,442
In NASA's photographs,
the lakes are full in 2002.

86
00:05:38,825 --> 00:05:43,527
By 2012, they had mostly dried
up, as the Nile's flow dropped.

87
00:05:43,841 --> 00:05:48,496
In 2021, after floods upstream,
they held more water than ever before.

88
00:05:48,775 --> 00:05:51,016
So yes, we can green the desert.

89
00:05:51,341 --> 00:05:56,484
One field at a time, with water stored
long ago and only slowly replaced.

90
00:05:57,181 --> 00:06:01,245
In 2009, three researchers
proposed going much bigger:

91
00:06:01,686 --> 00:06:06,411
forests of fast-growing trees
across the Sahara and the Australian Outback,

92
00:06:06,736 --> 00:06:09,244
watered with desalinated seawater.

93
00:06:09,755 --> 00:06:14,144
By their estimate, such forests
could take up about as much carbon each year

94
00:06:14,225 --> 00:06:16,431
as the world's fossil fuels release.

95
00:06:16,872 --> 00:06:21,876
That estimate came from plantation data
and calculations with large error bars,

96
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:24,952
not from a climate model
that simulated the carbon.

97
00:06:25,394 --> 00:06:27,588
And it wasn't carbon removal forever:

98
00:06:27,797 --> 00:06:32,127
the authors expected a forest to stop
gaining carbon after about a century.

99
00:06:32,569 --> 00:06:35,076
The authors themselves
described such projects

100
00:06:35,123 --> 00:06:37,990
as multi-trillion-dollar undertakings.

101
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:38,977
Would it rain?

102
00:06:39,673 --> 00:06:45,618
In the authors' own simulation, one climate
model, rainfall rose by more than 1,000

103
00:06:45,699 --> 00:06:51,481
millimeters a year, about 40 inches,
over roughly half of the irrigated Sahara.

104
00:06:51,922 --> 00:06:54,081
They asked other models to check it.

105
00:06:54,406 --> 00:06:58,841
A later team tested an irrigated Sahara
forest in a different climate model.

106
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:04,936
Across the whole Sahara, rainfall rose
by about 267 millimeters a year,

107
00:07:05,111 --> 00:07:10,776
roughly 10 inches, and the surface air
cooled by about 6.3 degrees Celsius,

108
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:13,029
around 11 degrees Fahrenheit.

109
00:07:13,539 --> 00:07:15,873
The authors called that a weak increase.

110
00:07:16,198 --> 00:07:20,505
Those two numbers measure different
things: rain over half the forest,

111
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,385
versus an average across the whole Sahara.

112
00:07:23,559 --> 00:07:25,706
And the forest wouldn't water itself.

113
00:07:26,032 --> 00:07:31,895
In that model, it lost about 1,238
millimeters of water a year to evaporation

114
00:07:31,941 --> 00:07:37,015
and transpiration, and total
rainfall over the Sahara was 319,

115
00:07:37,258 --> 00:07:38,826
about a quarter as much.

116
00:07:39,151 --> 00:07:42,645
That's a ratio of totals,
not a tracked drop of water.

117
00:07:42,970 --> 00:07:46,082
Much of the moisture
was carried south, over the Sahel,

118
00:07:46,256 --> 00:07:50,946
the semi-arid band just south
of the Sahara, and the nearby ocean.

119
00:07:51,643 --> 00:07:57,227
Keeping the forest alive took
about 919 millimeters, roughly 36 inches,

120
00:07:57,355 --> 00:08:00,502
of desalinated water
a year on top of the rain.

121
00:08:00,827 --> 00:08:04,890
The model simply assumes that water
is supplied; whether it could be produced

122
00:08:04,937 --> 00:08:08,687
and delivered at that scale
is not something the simulation shows.

123
00:08:09,012 --> 00:08:11,949
That's one simulation, not a measurement.

124
00:08:12,274 --> 00:08:15,385
But the biggest changes
may not be in the Sahara at all.

125
00:08:15,827 --> 00:08:18,160
Now, the part that's easy to miss.

126
00:08:18,161 --> 00:08:19,694
First, dust.

127
00:08:20,495 --> 00:08:25,986
NASA satellite data suggest that,
on average, about 182 million tons of dust

128
00:08:26,044 --> 00:08:31,118
a year are carried past the Sahara's
western edge, and out over the Atlantic.

129
00:08:31,559 --> 00:08:35,344
Roughly 28 million tons of it
settle on the Amazon basin.

130
00:08:35,785 --> 00:08:40,661
That dust carries an estimated
22,000 tons of phosphorus a year,

131
00:08:40,986 --> 00:08:44,655
about as much as the rainforest
loses to rain and floods.

132
00:08:45,038 --> 00:08:46,257
Where does it come from?

133
00:08:46,698 --> 00:08:52,051
The Bodélé Depression in Chad is one
of the world's most prolific dust sources:

134
00:08:52,376 --> 00:08:55,592
the dry floor of old Lake Mega-Chad,

135
00:08:55,836 --> 00:09:00,491
where the dust is mostly quartz
and the fossil remains of tiny algae.

136
00:09:01,002 --> 00:09:04,148
But it may not be the main
supplier to the Amazon.

137
00:09:04,531 --> 00:09:10,302
A newer analysis points to El Djouf,
a desert in Mauritania and Mali,

138
00:09:10,604 --> 00:09:15,840
about 2,500 kilometers,
1,600 miles, to the west.

139
00:09:16,061 --> 00:09:17,419
So here's the open question.

140
00:09:17,976 --> 00:09:22,818
If a greener Sahara blew less dust,
would the Amazon get less phosphorus?

141
00:09:23,119 --> 00:09:27,671
That's a hypothesis, and we found no study
that models it for a planted Sahara.

142
00:09:28,181 --> 00:09:30,074
Second, storms.

143
00:09:30,770 --> 00:09:35,786
Researchers simulated the world about 6,000
years ago, when the Sahara was green.

144
00:09:36,297 --> 00:09:40,906
In their model the Sahara was covered
in shrubs, not an artificial forest,

145
00:09:41,150 --> 00:09:43,762
and dust was cut by up to 80 percent.

146
00:09:44,458 --> 00:09:48,324
Compared with the same model's
pre-industrial world, that produced

147
00:09:48,382 --> 00:09:53,514
more tropical cyclone activity in both
hemispheres, especially around the Caribbean

148
00:09:53,607 --> 00:09:58,483
and the southeastern United States,
though some ocean regions saw fewer.

149
00:09:59,296 --> 00:10:03,661
A stronger West African monsoon
changed winds across the tropics.

150
00:10:03,870 --> 00:10:08,503
That's a simulation of the past,
not a forecast for a planted Sahara,

151
00:10:08,712 --> 00:10:11,823
and there aren't enough records
of ancient storms to check it.

152
00:10:12,334 --> 00:10:15,097
But it shows how far the Sahara reaches.

153
00:10:15,097 --> 00:10:19,787
Meanwhile, something real has been happening
on the Sahara's southern edge.

154
00:10:20,171 --> 00:10:25,337
After the droughts of the 1970s and 80s,
satellite records show vegetation returning

155
00:10:25,383 --> 00:10:29,807
to the Sahel as the rains came
back in the 1990s and 2000s.

156
00:10:30,086 --> 00:10:34,950
Researchers concluded that those large-scale
changes are mainly driven by rainfall,

157
00:10:35,101 --> 00:10:38,654
and that lasting desertification
was limited to local areas.

158
00:10:38,932 --> 00:10:43,994
Separately, UNCCD documents credit
farmers who protect trees sprouting

159
00:10:44,053 --> 00:10:47,826
on their own land with restoring
more than five million hectares

160
00:10:47,895 --> 00:10:52,389
in southern Niger's Maradi
region, about 12 million acres,

161
00:10:52,714 --> 00:10:57,961
and over seven million hectares
across Niger, more than 17 million acres.

162
00:10:58,402 --> 00:11:02,605
Those are reported figures,
and they differ by region and report.

163
00:11:03,046 --> 00:11:07,168
In 2007, the African Union
launched the Great Green Wall.

164
00:11:07,493 --> 00:11:12,241
Its stated ambition, a pledge made
at the UN climate conference known as COP21,

165
00:11:12,566 --> 00:11:17,025
is to restore 100 million hectares
of degraded land by 2030.

166
00:11:17,535 --> 00:11:21,657
The Wall's own coordinating agency
has set a separate, smaller target:

167
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:23,758
25 million hectares.

168
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:28,797
By 2020, a UN report, based on figures
reported to the Wall's own agency,

169
00:11:29,006 --> 00:11:32,977
counted about 4 million hectares
restored inside its core zones.

170
00:11:33,302 --> 00:11:37,273
The project has shifted
from a literal wall of trees to a mosaic

171
00:11:37,342 --> 00:11:42,091
of different land uses, including regrown
vegetation and water-holding measures.

172
00:11:42,416 --> 00:11:44,912
So, could we turn the Sahara green?

173
00:11:45,237 --> 00:11:46,967
In patches, we already are.

174
00:11:47,176 --> 00:11:49,498
All of it would take water we'd
have to make from the sea,

175
00:11:49,545 --> 00:11:52,354
and a great deal of energy
to desalinate and pump it.

176
00:11:52,679 --> 00:11:55,628
The simulations disagree
about how much rain it would bring.

177
00:11:55,872 --> 00:11:59,308
And what it would do far from Africa
is still an open question.

178
00:11:59,308 --> 00:12:00,457
And here's the twist.

179
00:12:00,782 --> 00:12:03,789
Orbital cycles turned
the Sahara green before.

180
00:12:04,115 --> 00:12:07,586
According to NASA, it's unclear
whether, and how much,

181
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:12,021
the Sahara might green again
over the coming centuries and millennia,

182
00:12:12,404 --> 00:12:15,910
now that greenhouse gases
are layered on top of those cycles.

183
00:12:16,235 --> 00:12:19,544
So the real question isn't
whether the desert can be green.

184
00:12:19,823 --> 00:12:22,609
It's whether we should be the ones
to flip the switch.

185
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:27,357
If you want to see what else satellites
have caught changing, subscribe.
